 ## Overview
-Near-Far Linear Ray Generation generates a list of 3D sampling points along a camera ray segment defined by near and far clipping distances [S1, S2, S4, unofficial]. It provides a structured set of depths along a ray for volume rendering evaluation [S1, S2, S4, unofficial]. It differs from [Near-Middle-Far Ray Generation](6817-near-middle-far-ray-generation.md) by using uniform or disparity-based spacing across the entire near-far interval rather than splitting the ray into distinct regions, and from [Refined Ray Generation via PDF Sampling](6837-refined-ray-generation-via-pdf-sampling.md) by relying on fixed geometric spacing rather than learned probability distributions [S2, S3, unofficial].
+Near-Far Linear Ray Generation generates a list of 3D sampling points along a camera ray segment defined by near ($t_{min}$) and far ($t_{max}$) distances for a specified number of samples [S1, S2, S3, S4, unofficial]. It solves the problem of discretizing the continuous ray domain into a finite set of query points for volume rendering or neural radiance field evaluation. It sets itself apart from its siblings by providing the foundational uniform or disparity-based spacing logic upon which stratified, probabilistic, or depth-guided variants build [S2, S3, S4, unofficial]. Unlike [Inverse CDF Ray Sampling](11412-inverse-cdf-ray-sampling.md) which adapts density to learned probabilities, or [Depth-Guided Supersampling](12190-depth-guided-supersampling.md) which uses external depth priors, this method relies on fixed geometric intervals [S2, S3, S4, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs:**
-- Ray origin $\mathbf{o}$ and direction $\mathbf{d}$ [S3, S4, unofficial].
-- Near distance $t_{near}$ and far distance $t_{far}$ along the ray [S1, S2, S4, unofficial].
-- Number of samples $N$ (often denoted `num_points` or `N_samples`) [S1, S3, unofficial].
-- Boolean flag `lindisp`: if `True`, samples in disparity space ($1/z$); if `False`, samples in linear depth space [S3, unofficial].
-- Float `perturb` (or `jitter`): magnitude of random noise added to break sampling symmetry [S3, S4, unofficial].
+- `rays_o`: Ray origins, shape $(N_{rays}, 3)$ [S3, S4, unofficial].
+- `rays_d`: Ray directions, shape $(N_{rays}, 3)$ [S3, S4, unofficial].
+- `near`: Near plane distances, shape $(N_{rays}, 1)$ [S3, S4, unofficial].
+- `far`: Far plane distances, shape $(N_{rays}, 1)$ [S3, S4, unofficial].
+- `N_samples`: Integer, number of depth samples per ray [S3, unofficial].
+- `lindisp`: Boolean. If `True`, samples in disparity space ($1/z$); if `False`, samples in linear depth space [S3, S4, unofficial].
+- `perturb`: Float. Amount of random jitter to add to samples (0.0 for no jitter) [S3, S4, unofficial].
+- `bbox_3D`: Optional 3D Axis-Aligned Bounding Box (AABB) for clipping [S4, unofficial].
 
 **Outputs:**
-- Array of 3D coordinates $\mathbf{x}_i$ for $i=0 \dots N-1$ [S1, S3, unofficial].
-- Array of depth values $z_i$ (or $t_i$) corresponding to the samples [S2, S3, unofficial].
+- `xyz_coarse_sampled`: 3D coordinates of sampled points, shape $(N_{rays}, N_{samples}, 3)$ [S3, S4, unofficial].
 
 **Data Structures:**
-- `rays_o`: Tensor of shape $(N_{rays}, 3)$ containing ray origins [S3, unofficial].
-- `rays_d`: Tensor of shape $(N_{rays}, 3)$ containing ray directions [S3, unofficial].
-- `near`: Tensor of shape $(N_{rays}, 1)$ containing near distances [S3, unofficial].
