 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Near-Far Clipping](7709-near-far-clipping.md)
-Post-processing step that constrains ray sampling intervals to a specified viewing volume using fixed global near and far distances. It prevents sampling empty space beyond the far plane or artifacts near the camera origin. Unlike [Inverse CDF Ray Sampling](11412-inverse-cdf-ray-sampling.md) which adapts density to scene content, or [Near-Far Linear/Disparity Ray Generation with Stratification](11841-near-far-linear-disparity-ray-generation-with-stratification.md) which handles stratified intervals, this method applies rigid, uniform bounds to all rays. Choose it when strict geometric bounds are required without adaptive sampling.
+Constrains ray sampling intervals to a specified viewing volume defined by fixed global near and far distances. Ensures samples are only generated within valid bounds, preventing rays from sampling empty space beyond the far plane or artifacts near the camera origin. Applies rigid, uniform bounds to all rays.
 
 ### [Inverse CDF Ray Sampling](11412-inverse-cdf-ray-sampling.md)
-Samples points along rays using inverse CDF sampling based on probability distributions associated with depth intervals. It maps uniform random noise to depth intervals using the cumulative distribution function derived from per-hit probabilities. It builds on [Near-Far Linear Ray Generation](6841-near-far-linear-ray-generation.md) by replacing uniform spacing with probability-weighted stratification. Choose it when sampling density should adapt to learned or computed probability distributions rather than fixed geometry.
+Samples points along rays using inverse CDF sampling based on probability distributions associated with depth intervals. Maps uniform random noise to depth intervals using the cumulative distribution function derived from per-hit probabilities. Replaces uniform spacing with probability-weighted stratification.
 
 ### [Near-Far Linear/Disparity Ray Generation with Stratification](11841-near-far-linear-disparity-ray-generation-with-stratification.md)
-Generates sample points along camera rays by sampling depths either linearly or in disparity space, with optional stratification to reduce variance. It extends [Near-Far Linear Ray Generation](6841-near-far-linear-ray-generation.md) by supporting disparity-based spacing and depth-guided supersampling, distinguishing it from [Inverse CDF Ray Sampling](11412-inverse-cdf-ray-sampling.md) which uses probabilistic distributions rather than fixed geometric spacing. Choose it when variance reduction via stratification is needed without full probabilistic sampling.
+Generates sample points along camera rays by sampling z-values (depths) either linearly or in disparity space, with optional stratification to reduce variance. Extends the base method by supporting disparity-based spacing and depth-guided supersampling.
 
 ### [Depth-Guided Supersampling](12190-depth-guided-supersampling.md)
-Generates sample positions along a ray by perturbing a depth estimate with Gaussian noise, encouraging the renderer to sample around the estimated surface location. It differs from standard linear spacing by focusing computational effort on likely geometry, and from Inverse CDF sampling by relying on an external depth prior rather than a learned density distribution. Choose it when a depth prior is available to concentrate samples on geometry.
+Generates sample positions along a ray by perturbing a depth estimate with Gaussian noise, encouraging the renderer to sample around the estimated surface location. Relies on an external depth prior rather than a learned density distribution.
 
 ### [Dataset-Specific Near/Far Bound Calculation](12599-dataset-specific-near-far-bound-calculation.md)
-Computes the near and far clipping planes for ray sampling based on the dataset type and Normalized Device Coordinates (NDC) configuration. It ensures that the ray sampler operates within a geometrically valid range appropriate for the scene's scale and projection model, serving as the parameterization layer for [Near-Far Linear Ray Generation](6841-near-far-linear-ray-generation.md). Choose it when adapting sampling bounds to specific dataset conventions or NDC projections.
+Computes the near and far clipping planes for ray sampling based on the dataset type and Normalized Device Coordinates (NDC) configuration. Ensures that the ray sampler operates within a geometrically valid range appropriate for the scene's scale and projection model.
 
 ### [Uniform Ray Marching with Occupancy Filtering](13089-uniform-ray-marching-with-occupancy-filtering.md)
-Samples a fixed number of points along each ray globally, then filters out samples that fall outside of occupied Sparse Point Cloud (SPC) cells. It differs from its sibling [SPC Uniform Raymarching](11412-spc-uniform-raymarching.md), which calculates step sizes based on cell geometry, and [SPC Voxel Raymarching](11413-spc-voxel-raymarching.md), which samples a fixed number of points *within* each intersected cell. This method prioritizes a uniform global sampling density, using the SPC structure solely as a validity mask to discard empty-space samples. Choose it when a uniform global density is desired but empty-space filtering is needed.
+Samples a fixed number of points along each ray globally, then filters out samples that fall outside of occupied Sparse Point Cloud (SPC) cells. Prioritizes a uniform global sampling density, using the SPC structure solely as a validity mask.
+
+### [Near-Far Linear Ray Generation with Stratified Sampling](14590-near-far-linear-ray-generation-with-stratified-sampling.md)
+Generates ray samples along a near-far segment by dividing the depth range into equal intervals and drawing one uniform random sample from each interval, ensuring low-discrepancy coverage. Guarantees that every sub-interval contributes exactly one sample, reducing variance.
 
 ### Near-Far Disparity Linear Ray Generation with Jitter
-Generates ray samples using disparity spacing with random jitter within segments, similar to NeRF's sampling but with explicit segment length calculation [S2, unofficial]. It creates linear $tvals$ from 0 to 1, converts them to disparity space using $tvals = 1. / (1. / near * (1 - tvals) + 1. / far * tvals)$, and if jitter > 0, computes midpoints, upper/lower bounds, and random $t_{rand}$ to jitter $tvals$ within segments: $tvals = lower + (upper - lower) * t_{rand}$ [S2, unofficial]. It computes segment lengths by differencing $tvals$, appending a large value ($1e10$), and multiplying by ray direction norm, then returns ray positions as $raypos = campos + raydir * tvals$ [S2, unofficial].
+Generates ray samples using disparity spacing with random jitter within segments, similar to NeRF's sampling but with explicit segment length calculation [S2, unofficial]. It creates linear $t_{vals}$ from 0 to 1 with `point_count` steps, converts them to disparity space using $t_{vals} = 1. / (1. / \text{near} \cdot (1 - t_{vals}) + 1. / \text{far} \cdot t_{vals})$, and applies jitter if `jitter > 0` by computing midpoints, upper/lower bounds, and random $t_{rand}$ to jitter $t_{vals}$ [S2, unofficial]. Segment lengths are computed by differencing $t_{vals}$, appending a large value ($1e10$), and multiplying by ray direction norm [S2, unofficial]. Ray positions are computed directly from $t_{vals}$ as $\text{raypos} = \text{campos} + \text{raydir} \cdot t_{vals}$ [S2, unofficial].
 
 ### Linear Seed Line Generation
-A method to generate seed points along a line segment between `m_startPt` and `m_endPt` [S5, unofficial]. It calculates `delta` as `(m_endPt - m_startPt) / count` and loops `i` from 0 to `count - 1`, setting `m_h_seeds[i]` to `m_startPt + i * delta` [S5, unofficial]. The parameter `count` specifies the number of seed points to generate [S5, unofficial].
+A method to generate seed points along a line segment between `m_startPt` and `m_endPt` [S5, unofficial]. It calculates `delta` as $(\text{m\_endPt} - \text{m\_startPt}) / \text{count}$ and loops $i$ from 0 to `count - 1`, setting $\text{m\_h\_seeds}[i] = \text{m\_startPt} + i \cdot \text{delta}$ [S5, unofficial]. The parameter `count` specifies the number of seed points to generate [S5, unofficial].
 
 ### Near-Far Linear Ray Generation with NDC Depth Simulation
-Generates samples in NDC space by initializing sample count $N_{samples}$, retrieving near/far planes, and generating linearly spaced depth values `interpx` between near and far [S6, unofficial]. If training, it adds uniform random noise to `interpx` scaled by step size $(far-near)/N_{samples}$ [S6, unofficial]. It converts linear depth to NDC depth using $ndc\_depth = 2 * ndc\_near\_plane / (1 - ndc\_near\_plane - interpx)$ and rescales to simulated Euclidean depth [S6, unofficial]. It computes ray points using either simulated or linear depth, and masks out points outside the AABB defined by `self.aabb[0]` and `self.aabb[1]` [S6, unofficial]. The parameter `ndc_near_plane` is typically 1.0 [S6, unofficial].
-
-### Near-Far Linear Ray Generation with Stratified Sampling
-Generates stratified samples along a ray segment by dividing the interval into equal parts and adding uniform random noise to each sample's t-value [S7, unofficial]. It computes step size $dt$ as $(t_{max} - t_{min}) / num\_samples$, allocates a field for $num\_samples$ 3D positions, loops over sample index $i$ from 0 to $num\_samples-1$, computes $t$ with stratified noise: $t_{min} + dt * (i + random())$, and computes sample position: $ray\_origin + ray\_direction * t$ [S7, unofficial]. The parameter $num\_samples$ is the number of samples per ray, e.g., 384 [S7, unofficial].
+Generates samples in NDC space by initializing sample count $N_{samples}$, retrieving near/far clipping planes, and generating linearly spaced depth values `interpx` between near and far [S6, unofficial]. If training, uniform random noise is added to `interpx` scaled by step size $(\text{far}-\text{near})/N_{samples}$ [S6, unofficial]. Linear depth is converted to NDC depth using $\text{ndc\_depth} = 2 \cdot \text{ndc\_near\_plane} / (1 - \text{ndc\_near\_plane} - \text{interpx})$ [S6, unofficial]. The depth is then rescaled to simulated Euclidean depth using `util.grad_rescale(interpx, ndc_depth)` [S6, unofficial]. Ray points are computed using either simulated or linear depth, and a mask is generated to check if points are outside the AABB defined by `self.aabb[0]` and `self.aabb[1]` [S6, unofficial]. The parameter `ndc_near_plane` is typically 1.0 [S6, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(N_{rays} \cdot N_{samples})$ for generating sample positions [S1, S3, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_{rays} \cdot N_{samples})$ to store the sampled coordinates and depths [S3, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Simple to implement, deterministic (without jitter), and provides uniform coverage of the near-far range [S3, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Uniform sampling may waste samples in empty space or undersample complex geometry [S3, unofficial]. It does not adapt to scene content [S2, unofficial].
-- **When to Use:** When a simple, uniform sampling strategy is sufficient, or as a baseline for more advanced sampling methods [S3, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(N_{rays} \cdot N_{samples})$ for generating depth values and 3D coordinates [S3, S4, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_{rays} \cdot N_{samples})$ to store the sampled points [S3, S4, unofficial].
+- **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources.
+- **Scaling:** Scales linearly with the number of rays and samples [S3, S4, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Simple to implement; provides uniform coverage of the ray segment; disparity sampling is beneficial for scenes with large depth variations [S3, S4, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** Uniform sampling may waste samples in empty space; disparity sampling can introduce precision issues near the camera [S3, S4, unofficial].
+- **When to use:** When a simple, uniform sampling strategy is sufficient; when disparity sampling is needed for deep scenes [S3, S4, unofficial].
+- **When not to use:** When the scene has complex geometry requiring adaptive sampling; when computational resources are limited and stratified or importance sampling is preferred [S3, S4, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Near-Far Linear Ray Generation is a fundamental technique in volume rendering [S1, S2, S3, unofficial]. It generates 3D sampling points along a ray segment defined by near and far distances [S1, S2, S4, unofficial], optionally using disparity spacing or jitter to break sampling symmetry [S2, S3, unofficial].
+Near-Far Linear Ray Generation is a fundamental technique in volume rendering and neural radiance fields (NeRF) for discretizing camera rays into sample points. It was popularized by the NeRF paper [S3, unofficial] and is widely used in various implementations such as TensoRF [S4, unofficial] and PointNeRF [S2, unofficial]. The method allows for efficient evaluation of neural networks along rays to render images or extract 3D structures [S1, S3, S4, unofficial].
 
